in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some texture compression  gurus solve their bucket creation  problems will we zip up their  performance stay tuned to find out  encode alamode came to us with a small  problem their cloud-based texture  compression service was handling 23,000  encodings a day and some of their  clients were starting to notice that the  time it took to export their data was  starting to get significantly longer  their system works something like this  for each build their script would create  a new bucket in cloud storage upload all  the textures from the client and then  kick off work to a load balancer we're a  bunch of compute engine instances would  start converting textures their setup  required that for each batch of textures  to be uploaded a unique bucket was  created so they can more easily track  the progress of encoding state although  their setup was pretty sound they  couldn't ignore their customers notes  about performance so it was time to dig  in and figure out the problem our first  step was to check the load balancing and  scaling operation if everything so is  setup right new instances should be spun  up properly so to check that out we  looked at the CPU utilization for the  instance group the values were low about  in the 12% range and there was only  about three to four instances running so  the problem was not that the instances  were getting overloaded now a CPU rates  that low the next thing we had to check  to make sure was that this machines  weren't getting stalled crashing or  hitting a weird error condition but  according to the logs they were  receiving data right on time and in fact  most of the instances in the group were  sitting around just waiting for work  with the instance group removed as a  potential source of the problem we set  our gaze to the GCS bucket and how the  team was using it and lo and behold ran  right into the issue it turns out that  there is a per project rate limit to  bucket creation and deletion of  approximately one operation every two  seconds according to the documentation  for them this meant that if 30 artists  across multiple companies all tried to  export at the same time then random  folks would start getting exception 429  to be thrown signaling that the rate was  exceeded for bucket creation where this  turned into a performance problem was  that the Python API their tool was using  had to implement an exponential back-off  and reach rot which is a nice and common  practice but since the queue was getting  backed up folks were ending up waiting a  longer  I'm for a simple export turns out they  didn't need that much dynamic bucket  creation instead they created one bucket  for each artists ads they registered  with the system this removed the entire  bucket creation bottleneck making things  a lot faster and made it easier to  handle tracking down what assets were  causing problems in the case of an error  to learn more about the perf details of  these buckets check out the article  linked in the description below  and don't forget to check out the rest  of our cloud performance Atlas content  because when it comes to performance  every millisecond counts
 

